EXT. HOT-AIR BALLOON - OVER THE TANEZROUFT DESERT - DAY

The envelope is half-collapsed, patched with a dozen mismatched swatches of canvas. The wicker basket drags a shadow across a sea of red dunes that goes on to every horizon. No road. No river. A wind that tastes of iron.

PILOT MAREN COLE, 50s, leather helmet pushed back, sun-cracked lips, sits against the burner and rations water with a bottle cap. Opposite her, a BOY, 12, thin as a pole in a man's flight jacket, stares at the horizon without blinking.

MAREN
Drink.

BOY
You first.

MAREN
I had mine.

BOY
You didn't.

MAREN
Then drink half and give me the rest.

The Boy sips. Hands her the bottle. She does not drink, just holds it.

BOY
What's your name?

MAREN
Maren.

BOY
I'm not going to call you that.

MAREN
You can call me whatever you like. What do I call you?

BOY
Nothing yet.

MAREN
That's a bad name.

The balloon dips, then lifts again. The burner coughs. Maren jams a boot against the gas line and it steadies.

MAREN (CONT'D)
Propane's low. Forty minutes of flame, maybe less.

BOY
Then what?

MAREN
Then we go where the wind sends us.

BOY
Is that far?

MAREN
No farther than we've come.

The Boy shifts. A silver chain slides from his collar. A small tin medal on the end. Engraved: AMADI.

MAREN (CONT'D)
That's your name.

BOY
It's my brother's name.

MAREN
Where's your brother?

BOY
In the plane.

Maren looks at the dunes. A long way off, a thin black thread of smoke rises, flattens, wavers in the heat.

MAREN
Amadi.

The Boy looks at her.

MAREN (CONT'D)
That's a good name. Two syllables. Easy to shout.

AMADI
Don't.

MAREN
Why not?

AMADI
When you shout, somebody doesn't come.

Maren says nothing. She puts the bottle in his hands.

MAREN
Drink the rest, Amadi.

He drinks. She watches the smoke. The burner flames sputter, and for the first time a thin wind catches the envelope and turns the basket, very slowly, toward the thread of black.

AMADI
Maren.

MAREN
Yes.

AMADI
Is that where we are going?

MAREN
That is where the wind is going.

The flame dies. Silence. The basket swings. The desert slides under them like a tide.
